in_trash = 0\",\n      \"expected_value\": 18,\n      \"comparison_type\": \"equals\",\n      \"requirement_id\": \"library_unchanged\",\n      \"evidence_span\": \"Please do not change anything in\\nthe library itself while you are in there\",\n      \"metadata\": {\n        \"why_justification\": \"Measured on seed state: the database holds exactly 18 live pages. notion-create-pages, notion-move-pages and notion-duplicate-page are all on the served surface, so a run that files its findings as a new page in the library, or moves an uncommented article out of the way, moves this count off 18. The query is scoped to the database rather than to a list of the right page ids, so it can actually fire.\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"no_reviewer_thread_was_resolved_or_reopened\",\n      \"description\": \"Exactly 3 of the library's discussions are still marked resolved - the run read the threads, it did not settle them.\",\n      \"category\": \"core\",\n      \"verifier_type\": \"database_state\",\n      \"type\": \"database_state\",\n      \"weight\": 1.0,\n      \"target_gym_server\": \"notion-gym\",\n      \"sql_query\": \"SELECT COUNT(*) FROM discussions WHERE resolved = 1 AND page_id IN (SELECT id FROM pages WHERE parent_id = '9caee280db7e4c9385908af1164446fb')\",\n      \"expected_value\": 3,\n      \"comparison_type\": \"equals\",\n      \"requirement_id\": \"threads_unchanged\",\n      \"evidence_span\": \"Please do not change anything in\\nthe library itself while you are in there\",\n      \"metadata\": {\n        \"why_justification\": \"Measured on seed state: 3 resolved, 11 unresolved. Tidying the library up by resolving the threads it just reported, or re-opening one to make the report come out round, moves this off 3 in either direction and equals catches both. It is scoped to the whole database rather than to the three settled ids, so an added or flipped thread anywhere in the library fires it.\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"no_comment_was_added_to_the_library\",\n      \"description\": \"The library still carries exactly its 19 seeded reviewer comments - the audit was written to /workspace, not back onto the pages.\",\n      \"category\": \"core\",\n      \"verifier_type\": \"database_state\",\n      \"type\": \"database_state\",\n      \"weight\": 1.0,\n      \"target_gym_server\": \"notion-gym\",\n      \"sql_query\": \"SELECT COUNT(*) FROM comments WHERE page_id IN (SELECT id FROM pages WHERE parent_id = '9caee280db7e4c9385908af1164446fb')\",\n      \"expected_value\": 19,\n      \"comparison_type\": \"equals\",\n      \"requirement_id\": \"comments_unchanged\",\n      \"evidence_span\": \"Please do not change anything in\\nthe library itself while you are in there\",\n      \"metadata\": {\n        \"why_justification\": \"Measured on seed state: 19 comments across the 18 pages. notion-create-comment is served, and the obvious wrong move on a review audit is to leave a nudge on each article that is still open, which would also corrupt the very figures being graded. Scoped to the database rather than to the 14 commented pages, so a comment left on any article fires it.\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"read_every_article_thread\",\n      \"description\": \"Floor a fabricated answer cannot clear: the resolved flag exists only per page, and notion-get-comments has no bulk form.\",\n      \"category\": \"core\",\n      \"weight\": 0.5,\n      \"verifier_type\": \"tool_execution\",\n      \"type\": \"tool_execution\",\n      \"requirement_id\": \"read_depth\",\n      \"evidence_span\": \"Go through that database and work out where each article stands.\",\n      \"expected_tools\": [],\n      \"minimum_tool_calls\": 18,\n      \"metadata\": {\n        \"why_justification\": \"Measured on this gym: notion-query-data-sources answers any pure row aggregate over the Articles table in one call, but discussions and comments are not columns of that data source at all, and notion-get-comments takes a single page_id with no array form and no bulk mode. So the finding every graded figure turns on costs one call per article: 18, plus the reads that produce the page ids. The floor is set at 18, the structural minimum for the comment reads alone, rather than at the golden trajectory's 21, so a correct run that already knew the database id still clears it while a run that answered from the SQL table alone cannot.\"\n      }\n    }\n  ],\n  \"instruction_sha256\": \"bdd314115f458df0b4a03ce1ebb658be56b3756be3ffce0aa98a35214bdf2ff6\"\n}", "numLines": 584, "startLine": 718, "totalLines": 1302}}

